real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896141Z  	-- THE HOTTEST LOOKUP IN THE GAME, and it was a sequential scan. `withCharacter`/`readCharacter` open
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896282Z  	-- every authed request with `WHERE account_id = $1 AND alive` (§7.1 lazy accrual makes even a READ take
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896415Z  	-- it), and 78 further sites across src/ look a character up by account. The table carried indexes on
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896551Z  	-- name, lower(name), respect, lfg and seeking_mentor — every SECONDARY lookup — and none on the join
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896756Z  	-- key the request wrapper itself uses, which is the shape you get when the wrapper is written first and
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896847Z  	-- the indexes are added later, one reported slow page at a time.
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9896976Z  	-- Measured on real Postgres at 3,000 players: seq scan, 144 buffers, 0.62ms — and it is paid PER
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9897122Z  	-- REQUEST, so the server-wide total is quadratic in the playerbase exactly like the standing scan was.
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9897262Z  	-- PLAIN, not `WHERE alive`: the partial index measured marginally faster on the hot query (0.164ms vs
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9897393Z  	-- 0.193ms — noise at this size) and serves only the subset that carries `AND alive`, while this one
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9897536Z  	-- also serves the estate/chain paths that read a bloodline's DEAD rows (`ORDER BY alive DESC`) and the
real Postgres	Stop containers	2026-09-20T23:38:05.9897655Z  	-- `account_id IN (…)` batch reads. Coverage across 78 sites beats 0.03ms on one of them.
